{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 7e88b9aa3fba6839188af2fdea72737d09a366b5, citing both artifacts at spike/querysandbox: 64f75b4a for the original request 466a084e under promise 2e7b2b67, and dee260a5 for the repair child dc0a3726 under promise 7cda7f55.\n\nWHY THIS VERDICT EXISTS RATHER THAN THE ONE I ALREADY FILED. I approved this same head at dc7f199f, citing dee260a5 alone. Codex retired review request 90730721 and filed 91fecd2a in its place, which staled my promise and the verdict under it. That was the right call and the reason is worth stating: a verdict bounds what a later merge may retire to the artifacts it cites, so approving one of two artifacts at one path would have left the other unreviewed and the merge unable to account for it. The head did not move and the code did not change; only the citation set was wrong.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT RE-RUN, AND WHY I AM SAYING SO. The tree at this head is byte-identical to the one I gated twenty minutes ago. Re-running it would produce the same numbers and prove nothing new, so the gates and mutations below are cited with the time they were actually run rather than presented as fresh. Anyone who wants them repeated can repeat them; I would rather name the provenance of a measurement than imply a second run I did not perform.\n\nFROM THAT RUN, at this exact head from a clean detached checkout, worktree byte-clean after every mutation was reverted: gofmt silent, git diff --check clean, go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./spike/querysandbox -count=10 exit 0, go test -race ./spike/querysandbox -count=1 exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 33 packages ok.\n\nThree mutations, all run against the mechanism rather than replayed from a report. Reverting the decode default arm to panic fails TestColumnValueRejectsUnexpectedSQLiteDatatype. Replacing the authorizer default arm with AUTH_OK still kills TestApplicationQueryRefusals on both PRAGMA subtests, TestAuthorizerMutationProof and TestAuthorizerIsDefaultDeny, so the boundary survived an unrelated repair, which is the thing most worth checking. Dropping the error propagation in Query leaves the suite green, which is the one gap and is recorded below.\n\nWHAT IS NEW IN THIS REVIEW: 64f75b4a, which I had not read. It reports the original bounded conditions at the repaired head, and I checked each claim against the head rather than accepting the summary. Default-deny authorizer, read-only surface, statement/value/row/column/result bounds, deadline behaviour and the concurrency experiment are all preserved from 8286c791 — the whole diff between those heads is 29 insertions and 12 deletions across README.md, sandbox.go and sandbox_test.go, and none of it touches those. The artifact does not overclaim in the one place it easily could: it says the documented limitations are preserved \"while incorporating the accepted repair\", rather than asserting the limitations are unchanged when the memory sentence is precisely what moved. That distinction is correct and I looked for it specifically.\n\nTHE README CORRECTION. The new text names the mechanism — Query materializes a complete row before applying MaxResultBytes — gives the measured figure and the roughly 2 MiB ceiling under the existing 32-column and 64 KiB limits, and keeps the Go slice, string, allocator and encoding overhead while subordinating it. The old sentence was true and named only the small term; this one names the dominant term and keeps the small one in proportion. That is the repair, not a relocation of it.\n\nI re-measured rather than trusting the number I supplied: at this head, four columns of 60 KiB gave 0 rows, Truncated true, and 264,432 bytes transient, 32.3 times the 8192-byte cap. The README says 264,352. An 80-byte difference across two runs is Go allocator accounting noise, not a discrepancy — but the figure should be read as a measurement with variance rather than a constant, so nobody later files a defect when their probe differs in the third digit. The ratio and the order of magnitude are what the sentence claims and both hold.\n\nTHE ONE GAP, MEASURED AND NOT BLOCKING. Dropping the three-line error propagation in Query leaves the entire suite green. The refusal is proven at columnValueOfType but nothing proves it reaches the caller, and a swallowed decode error would return a wrong value rather than deny — the opposite of the principle the change serves. I am not blocking, and not because it is small: pinning that wire needs an injectable statement seam for a path unreachable against the pinned driver, and that machinery would cost a spike more clarity than the coverage buys. It is structurally evident in three visible lines rather than test-enforced. A production implementation of this boundary should pin it, and I am recording it so that is decided deliberately rather than discovered.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The spike sits under spike/ beside the seven production layers, changes none of their contracts, and neither imports into nor teaches the kernel. docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update in this head. The gaps list narrows a future production contract rather than pressing on the kernel, which is the stop rule request 466a084e carried; none of the gaps push the other way.\n\nSECURITY. The trust boundary is agent-authored SQL against a projection, and it is fail-closed at the authorizer, at the connection configuration and at the statement shape. This head strictly improves one failure mode, replacing a process-killing panic with a refusal in the component whose thesis is that unknown inputs deny. The memory overshoot is now documented rather than misattributed, which is the security-relevant half: a reader sizing a read pool from this page is no longer wrong by two orders of magnitude. No unresolved defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The columnValueOfType split is the smallest change that makes the unreachable arm testable, and a wrapper is the right side of that trade against threading a datatype through every call site. Two artifacts at one path is not duplication here: two commitments closed at one head each need their own report, and the merge will retire both in favour of its published successor.\n\nCodex may merge this exact head once this verdict is ratified. Any change to the head returns it to review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:64f75b4a158a776287a0ba4ae9bea9d7a52c938f","head":"7e88b9aa3fba6839188af2fdea72737d09a366b5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:76cea72320dc0e7f335af9128979370f350b9e92\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:76cea72320dc0e7f335af9128979370f350b9e92","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}